<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Achilles</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Achilles is the greatest Greek warrior of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> in Greek legend and the central figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>. The son of the hero Peleus and the sea-nymph Thetis, he killed the Trojan prince Hector and was himself killed by an arrow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He was worshipped as a hero, notably around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> at Olbia and on the island of Leuke, and the Molossian kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> claimed descent from his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoptolemus">Neoptolemus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/achilles.jpg" alt="Achilles" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jona Lendering · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achilles_fighting_against_Memnon_Leiden_Rijksmuseum_voor_Oudheden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In Greek myth Achilles was the son of Thetis, a Nereid, and Peleus, king of the Myrmidons. According to one account, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> had both courted Thetis until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prometheus">Prometheus</a> warned Zeus of a prophecy that she would bear a son greater than his father, and the two gods had her wed Peleus. Achilles was raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phthia">Phthia</a> with his companion Patroclus and educated by the centaur Chiron.</p><p>The story that Thetis dipped him in the river Styx, leaving him vulnerable only at the heel, comes from the Achilleid of Statius, written in the first century CE. No source before Statius mentions general invulnerability: in the Iliad Achilles is wounded at the elbow.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, Achilles came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> with 50 ships, each carrying 50 Myrmidons. On the way he wounded King Telephus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysia">Mysia</a>, a wound that would not heal until Achilles healed it, as an oracle had said. His most notable feat was the slaying of the Trojan prince Hector outside the gates of Troy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> does not present his death, but other sources agree that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> killed him near the end of the war with an arrow. His tomb in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troad">Troad</a> was venerated through antiquity by Thessalians, by Persian expeditionary forces, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a>. Dedications from Greek colonies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> attest a heroic cult from the sixth century BCE, and in the third century CE stelae at Olbia name an Achilles Pontarches, invoked as protector of the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a> wrote a trilogy about him, of which only fragments survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Achilles” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Beyond the Olympians, the Greek world was full of divine powers: nymphs of springs and trees, river gods, the Fates, the Furies, and heroes, mortals of the distant past such as Heracles, Theseus and <b>Achilles</b>, who were worshipped at their tombs and could help or harm the living.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Wall painting survives in the tombs: the scenes of feasting and sport at Tarquinia, and the François Tomb at Vulci, of the 4th century BCE, which sets <b>Achilles</b>’ sacrifice of Trojan prisoners beside local heroes, among them Macstrna freeing Caelius Vibenna.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>The poem opens with an invocation to the Muse, and its first word, mēnin, “wrath”, names its theme, the wrath of <b>Achilles</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>The Molossian kings claimed descent from Neoptolemus, son of <b>Achilles</b>, and were called Aeacids.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-epirus">Kingdom of Epirus</a></li><li><blockquote>A self-governing polis, it worshipped Apollo Delphinios and <b>Achilles</b>, and issued early cast bronze money shaped like dolphins.</blockquote><a href="/state/olbia">Olbia</a></li><li><blockquote>Pharsalus stood on a hill above the plain of the Enipeus in southern Thessaly, in the region Greek myth linked to <b>Achilles's</b> Phthia.</blockquote><a href="/state/pharsalus">Pharsalus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.96,26.24" aria-label="Troy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Achilles"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4754 4149h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4754 4149h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.96,26.24">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
